\subsection{Sensors}
\subsubsection{Radar}
Thirteen mmWave Frequency-modulated Continuous Wave (FMCW) radars (TI IWR1443) are employed. 
The radar operates over a 4~GHz bandwidth from 77~GHz to 81~GHz. 
These radars emit linearly frequency-modulated chirps and determine object range and velocity by comparing the transmitted and reflected signals. 
The onboard 3~transmit and 4~receive antennas enable object angle estimation. 
We use FMCW-MIMO technology to generate range–azimuth–elevation maps and convert them into 3D point-cloud data representing reflections from surrounding objects. 
The resulting point clouds, captured frame by frame in x–y–z coordinates, form spatio-temporal sequences for analysis. 
When saving the data, the current timestamp, x, y, z, and density are stored together.
Each radar has its own coordinate system, so coordinate transformation and time synchronization are conducted to merge all data.
% This process produces a more accurate, information-rich, and denser point cloud, which better represents the target’s position, shape, and motion state. A visualization of aggregated data from radars is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:sample_radar}.

